#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538023} :activity/id #uuid "39c86382-08ce-590d-8116-eedcd38c705c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048189 :topic/id #uuid "86b8f7ea-5908-5137-a377-052e6fc4f731" :topic/math-academy-id 315} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245b618d-7b28-597c-b19b-5c843eb6c7f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247741} {:answer/id #uuid "52fe1c8c-100b-5622-9e05-9387df536621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248173} {:answer/id #uuid "a0850bb9-1690-5ca5-b8a2-6fbde9a42a7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248858} {:answer/id #uuid "b7508ec8-5378-54d4-bd32-a71a1b47afaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249065} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaf321e-a0ba-506b-a6c1-81f09db011a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249065} :answer-field/id #uuid "835d8e84-7024-5e35-bbe8-a87537867361" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e7b39f3-407b-599d-a487-d698e967777b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114046" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{9}{81\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/81)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/81}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\n The only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{9})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{9}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{9}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=9\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (9\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a8560ad-d3a4-5e23-a9c1-ad08b689d611" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ed7e88-42a3-5874-867c-a70eb196b8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333661} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc52df5-2130-5724-8f9a-3900402db0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(9x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334237} {:answer/id #uuid "961ad6bb-bcc5-55c8-bf62-b1d0251abaec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(9x)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334325} {:answer/id #uuid "d33c5bc0-c46c-505d-825e-d5fb312cca88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{81}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334839} {:answer/id #uuid "d9456840-7b87-506e-b8e7-b50a98cb1b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334237} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae037e8-4b57-5061-96fb-95244295292c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea572027-5f6a-57b6-b534-919c7f5636f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-69477" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}=3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $9x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=9x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(9x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "567dadd3-ca44-5bf9-8b98-d51883d57900" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538060}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b1168a-ec72-53fd-aa75-2d246631fb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537232} {:answer/id #uuid "0d190ad0-5d86-58ee-99e2-6b1dcbd8e20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537250} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9a665e-71e9-5ec8-bf10-4366e938cdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537477} {:answer/id #uuid "b06e5be7-dbe2-5334-99a2-daeab5470c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537565} {:answer/id #uuid "c764df4f-a1d7-5e04-a973-c2800addafd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537250} :answer-field/id #uuid "5596b996-55c7-584d-a670-904b75102451" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "affed96b-3c3d-54a9-a279-f8432cb1a012" :question/math-academy-id "q-114073" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e5e11b8-5899-58e1-8dff-5ad152048b10" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538066}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17153736-08a5-511b-8d66-3076de3a56f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339331} {:answer/id #uuid "287af432-ac1d-5bb0-9cce-df095ab56481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(2x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339481} {:answer/id #uuid "3031610e-8402-5eae-b837-b04a952ccb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339548} {:answer/id #uuid "741561ed-033d-5aff-8ad7-766c99be57a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340158} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a5cb98-9b11-5ca7-8b96-437d604616ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(2x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340906}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339548} :answer-field/id #uuid "79900759-4114-5519-b212-5c813256836c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a6fafd98-c438-5a64-a87a-7a2d0a58fd7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73717" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2\\,\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}=4\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $2x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5a7421e-17cd-51fe-9d7d-db4bb3d13560" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4d6374a-a8f4-5012-ba14-79979bb09a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319994} {:answer/id #uuid "1222c3a4-de2b-5ccf-879e-6d55aee4c8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537260}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537260} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e46496-0573-5f7f-b6a6-15d10b4e8306" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88256796-f1d7-5511-b30e-ef033cdfdfae" :question/math-academy-id "q-351834" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3\\cdot (1-25{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(5x)}^{2}.$   \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "edde14b3-9900-568d-8442-2bcf6680ae81"}] :activity/title "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537209}]}
